sections.content:
**`fixedRate = 5000` — start every 5 seconds**
- Task takes 3s → 2s gap between runs
- Task takes 8s → next run starts immediately after previous finishes (no overlap on single thread; possible overlap with thread pool)
- Use for: metrics emission, health pings, time-regular polls

**`fixedDelay = 5000` — 5 seconds after previous finishes**
- Task takes 3s → 8s between starts (3 + 5)
- Guarantees no overlap regardless of pool size
- Use for: sequential queue draining, anything that must not run concurrently

**`cron = "0 0 8 * * MON-FRI"` — Spring 6-field cron**
- Fields: seconds, minutes, hours, day-of-month, month, day-of-week
- The leading `0` is seconds — Unix cron has only 5 fields
- Copy-pasting a Unix expression silently misfires

**`initialDelay`**
- Time to wait before the first execution
- Without it, `fixedRate` tasks fire immediately on startup
- Use when an external dependency may not be ready at startup

**Code example**

```java
@Component
@EnableScheduling
public class ScheduledJobs {

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000, initialDelay = 30000)
    public void emitMetrics() { /* ... */ }

    @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 5000)
    public void drainQueue() { /* ... */ }

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * MON-FRI")
    public void nightlyReport() { /* ... */ }
}
```

**Clustered execution warning**
- Every instance runs every scheduled task independently — no built-in coordination
- For at-most-once cluster-wide execution, use ShedLock or a Quartz clustered scheduler
- Without coordination, 5 instances run the same cleanup simultaneously — lock contention, duplicate processing

**Why the single-thread default is dangerous**
- One slow task blocks all others
- Silent failure mode — scheduled tasks run late or not at all
- No warning in logs

**Custom `TaskScheduler` with a pool**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
public class SchedulingConfig {

    @Bean
    public TaskScheduler taskScheduler() {
        ThreadPoolTaskScheduler scheduler = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
        scheduler.setPoolSize(4);
        scheduler.setThreadNamePrefix("scheduled-");
        scheduler.setErrorHandler(t -> log.error("Scheduled task failed", t));
        scheduler.setRejectedExecutionHandler(
            new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        return scheduler;
    }
}
```

**Why the error handler matters**
- Default behavior: exceptions in scheduled tasks are silently logged to stderr and forgotten
- Without an error handler, tasks can fail for weeks unnoticed
- Always wire to your monitoring system (Sentry, Datadog, PagerDuty)

**Property-based alternative**

```properties
spring.task.scheduling.pool.size=4
spring.task.scheduling.thread-name-prefix=scheduled-
```

- Configures the auto-configured scheduler
- No place for a custom error handler — programmatic config wins for production

**Monitoring with Actuator**

```properties
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=scheduledtasks,metrics
```

- `/actuator/scheduledtasks` lists every registered task with its schedule
- Verify cron expressions match expectations
- Combine with Micrometer `@Timed` for execution time and failure counts

**Why bounded matters**
- `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` creates a new thread per invocation — unbounded
- Under load: thousands of threads, native memory exhaustion, JVM crash
- Bounded pool with backpressure is the production requirement

**Custom executor**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig {

    @Bean("asyncExecutor")
    public Executor asyncExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(5);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(20);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setRejectedExecutionHandler(
            new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix("async-");
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }
}
```

**Use the named executor**

```java
@Service
public class NotificationService {

    @Async("asyncExecutor")
    public CompletableFuture<Boolean> send(Notification n) {
        // runs on the async thread pool
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(true);
    }
}
```

**Why CallerRunsPolicy is the safest rejection handler**
- When the queue is full, the task runs on the caller's thread
- Provides natural backpressure: caller slows down because it's doing the work
- Compared to `AbortPolicy` (throws, loses work) or `DiscardPolicy` (silently drops)
- The HTTP request thread becomes synchronous under load — propagates backpressure to the load balancer

**Property-based alternative**

```properties
spring.task.execution.pool.core-size=5
spring.task.execution.pool.max-size=20
spring.task.execution.pool.queue-capacity=100
```

- Easier but no place for a custom rejection handler — programmatic for production

**The problem**
- `@Async` does not propagate thread-local values across thread boundaries
- Spring Security `SecurityContext` — empty in async thread
- MDC logging context — lost; correlation IDs disappear
- Request-scoped beans — not accessible

**TaskDecorator implementation**

```java
public class ContextCopyingTaskDecorator implements TaskDecorator {
    @Override
    public Runnable decorate(Runnable runnable) {
        SecurityContext security = SecurityContextHolder.getContext();
        Map<String, String> mdc = MDC.getCopyOfContextMap();
        return () -> {
            try {
                SecurityContextHolder.setContext(security);
                if (mdc != null) MDC.setContextMap(mdc);
                runnable.run();
            } finally {
                SecurityContextHolder.clearContext();
                MDC.clear();
            }
        };
    }
}
```

**Wire it into the executor**

```java
executor.setTaskDecorator(new ContextCopyingTaskDecorator());
```

**Operational notes**
- `MDC.clear()` and `SecurityContextHolder.clearContext()` in `finally` are mandatory — pooled threads carry stale context otherwise
- The decorator captures context at submit-time on the calling thread; restores on the worker thread
- Without this, MDC `correlationId` disappears in async logs — debugging becomes painful

**The void async method trap**
- `void` return = no `Future` to check
- Exceptions silently logged to stderr by the default uncaught handler
- Background tasks can fail for weeks before anyone notices

**`AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler` for void methods**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAsync
public class AsyncConfig implements AsyncConfigurer {

    @Override
    public AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler getAsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
        return (ex, method, params) -> {
            log.error("Async method {} failed with args {}",
                method.getName(), Arrays.toString(params), ex);
            metricsRegistry.counter("async.errors",
                "method", method.getName()).increment();
        };
    }
}
```

**Always prefer CompletableFuture<T> over void**

```java
@Async("asyncExecutor")
public CompletableFuture<EnrichedOrder> enrich(Order order) {
    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(externalApi.enrich(order));
}

enrich(order)
    .exceptionally(ex -> {
        log.error("Enrichment failed", ex);
        return Order.empty();
    })
    .thenAccept(this::persist);
```

- Caller has explicit control via `.exceptionally()` and `.handle()`
- Errors propagate to the caller's chain
- Composable with other `CompletableFuture` operations

**Testing async methods**
- `@SpringBootTest` loads the async infrastructure
- Use `CompletableFuture.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` to assert results — never `Thread.sleep()`
- Sleep-based tests are timing-flaky; `get()` with timeout is deterministic

**`@Order` for sequencing**
- Lower values run first
- Without `@Order`, execution order is undefined and may vary between restarts
- Use it whenever you have more than one runner and the order matters

**Code example**

```java
@Component
@Order(1)
public class DatabaseMigrationRunner implements CommandLineRunner {
    private final FlywayService flyway;

    public DatabaseMigrationRunner(FlywayService flyway) {
        this.flyway = flyway;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        flyway.migrate();
    }
}

@Component
@Order(2)
public class CacheWarmupRunner implements ApplicationRunner {
    private final ProductCache cache;

    public CacheWarmupRunner(ProductCache cache) { this.cache = cache; }

    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        if (!args.containsOption("skip-warmup")) {
            cache.preload();
        }
    }
}
```

**Failure semantics**
- An exception in any runner aborts startup
- `ApplicationFailedEvent` fires instead of `ApplicationReadyEvent`
- Context is closed; the JVM exits
- The web server already bound the port — load balancer briefly sees an open port that immediately closes

**Non-fatal failures**
- Catch the exception inside the runner and log it
- Decide consciously: should this failure block startup or not?
- For non-critical post-startup tasks, prefer `@EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)` so failure doesn't block readiness
